    {
      "id": "function-language-kernel-fidelity",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The function body language states what the Strata kernel computes without stand-ins: an AST field typed string or an id or scalar over string (has_role's role-id) is read as a name, so has_role evaluates and checks; a variant, enum value or field named like a keyword (value.null, expr.if, then, else) is written after a dot, as a match pattern and before a colon, and a keyword-named field is never bound by shorthand; the built-in unit type has the one value () (null in examples), and unit is not the representation of an id or scalar and cannot be declared again; print(L, e) writes an expression as the kernel's Display writes it; format's {:?} writes a value as Rust's derived Debug of the type specc generates and {:json} as serde_json's compact text (strings escaped, f64 as ryu writes it); startsWith, endsWith, length, trim, chars, snakeCase, parseTime and the ASCII character classes behave as the kernel's Rust (str::starts_with, chars().count(), str::trim over White_Space, transition.rs snake_case, plain.rs parse_time, char::is_ascii_*); a function calls itself only on a strict sub-value of a union parameter; and the language evaluator and checker give the kernel's results and exact error texts on the shared case table.",
      "rationale": "WG-4957 (with WG-4962): stating the kernel's functions (WG-4936, WG-4937, WG-4938) needed workarounds: has_role always false because the evaluator read only fields typed plain string, an absent parameter standing for Value::Null, the printed conditions and the blank-name test passed in as inputs, Debug texts spelled out per type, starts_with read through the rule language, JSON text without escapes, a list of lists named without recursion, and example error texts in Exegra's wording. Evidence is the core tests: the function fixture states holds-role, role-declared, print-rule, request-text, debug-texts, json-text, mentions-field, is-field-id, kind-rule, blank, route-ok, parse-time and the keyword-named plain-value functions with examples from the kernel's behaviour and its tests, each new rule has a negative fixture refused once with its code, and the shared case table gives the kernel's eval and check results and texts with each expression's printed source. Supported, not verified: Exegra does not check its own evaluator against the kernel; the kernel's tests read the same table.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
        "validator"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "capability-model-closed",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Capability, plan, role, grant, delegation, tokenScope and featureFlag are entity kinds, and a contract auth names the capabilities it requires in requires; every capability a contract, plan, role, grant, delegation, token scope or flag names is a capability (capability.unknown); with system.generation set every contract that is not local requires at least one (capability.required); every capability is conferred by some role or grant and is core or entitled by some plan (capability.unreachable); a delegation passes on only capabilities its source (role, token scope, grant or delegation) holds and a grant confers only capabilities its issuer role holds (delegation.exceeds); and a grant or delegation maxDuration is a positive duration (capability.duration).",
      "rationale": "Evidence is the core tests of WG-4954 (tests/core/capabilities.test.js): a specification whose contracts require capabilities that roles confer and plans entitle validates clean, a missing requires is accepted only without the switch or on a local contract, and each rule is reported once at its position by a fixture that breaks only it. Supported, not verified, because Exegra does not check its own validation rules.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
