      }
    },
    {
      "id": "example-fixture-rules",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A specification may declare example fixtures (section exampleFixtures, entity exampleFixture: id, type, value, description) and a function example's argument, result or error may be {fixture: id} or {fixture: id, with: {dotted path: value}} instead of the value; Exegra resolves the reference, and the overrides in order, before it evaluates the example, so the example is checked exactly as if the value were written out. Validation refuses a reference to a fixture that is not declared (example.fixture-unknown); a fixture whose type does not resolve or whose value is not of its type, and a fixture that, with its overrides, is not the type of the parameter, result or error it stands for (example.fixture-type); and an override path that does not lead into the fixture's value through struct fields, map keys, list indexes and union variant names, an empty step, or a with that is not an object (example.fixture-override).",
      "rationale": "WG-4963: every function example of Strata carried the whole test tenant and schema, which grew its specification from 3.3 MB to 5.2 MB and slowed every apply. Declaring such values once and referring to them removes the repetition without changing what an example states, and specc writes each fixture once per generated test module. Evidence is the core tests: the passing fixture uses fixtures with and without overrides and every example holds, and for each code a minimal specification breaking exactly that rule is refused once with that code. Supported, not verified: Exegra does not check its own validation rules.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
        "validator"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "function-branch-coverage",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "Exegra records which branches of each function its examples take (both branches of if, each arm of match, the value and the error path of try where the called function can fail, and fail), without changing what the examples evaluate to; exegra cover-functions reports per function the branches covered out of the total and each uncovered branch with its place in the body or let; an uncovered branch is reported and raises no readiness gap, because a rule that asks for one example per branch rewards examples that restate the function rather than its requirement; system.generation.functionBranches is still accepted and changes nothing.",
      "rationale": "WG-4965 made an uncovered branch a readiness gap; WG-5377 made it informational (Strata docs/assurance.md, principle 4: tests are judged by whether they fail when the code is wrong, not by branch coverage of examples). Evidence is tests/core/functions.test.js. Supported, not verified: Exegra does not check its own validation rules.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
        "agent-cli"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "format-gaps-rules",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "A contract may be a target (realisation target: specified, not implemented, served by no route, never loaded, committed or lowered onto by a derivation or facade); a derivation of a read or command may state header: true (the load with no needs first, its outputs readable by the needs function, then the needs loaded in one round that no need round counts); an untagged union (encoding tag untagged) is written as the raw JSON of its variant, read in declaration order, and validation refuses one with a tagField, contentField or variantCase, two variants written as one JSON shape or a json newtype before another variant (type.encoding); binding.suppliedBy says an input comes from the route or session and never the body, and validation refuses one naming no input or no such supplier (contract.supplied-by), and a form field or argument (form.supplied-input) or action argument (action.supplied-input) that fills one; a function body may write the JSON null where a json value is expected and nowhere else (function.expr-type), and a union variant named like a keyword (plain-json.null) after a dot of a path or in a match pattern, eval may take a lookup that following a reference reads, and sortBy orders lists item by item and unions by variant and fields, as the kernel's Value does.",
      "rationale": "WG-4968, from the read and view functions of Strata (WG-4939): CT-093 is target, the generic records contract takes raw JSON, tenant and context come from the route and session, projections write the JSON null, a query filter follows references and sorts lists, and the store loads a header before its needs. Evidence is the core tests: a fixture using each construct validates and its function examples hold, each rule has a minimal specification breaking exactly that rule, and values round-trip through their wire form. Supported, not verified: Exegra does not check its own validation rules.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
        "validator"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "language-checked-arithmetic",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The primitive catalogue holds checked integer arithmetic (add-checked, sub-checked, mul-checked), time arithmetic (add-checked and sub-checked on a timestamp and an int, time-difference, days-ms, hours-ms, minutes-ms) and text (concat-checked, to-text), and the language evaluator gives for them the results and exact error texts of the shared case table: every integer operand and result lies in -(2^53-1) to 2^53-1, and outside it the evaluation fails with integer-overflow (never a wrapped, saturated or rounded value), at both ends of the range and for a product beyond 64 bits; a concatenation longer than 4096 bytes of UTF-8 fails with string-too-long; a null operand gives null; an operand of the wrong kind is the error <op> does not apply to a <kind>; and the checker refuses an operand of the wrong shape (a timestamp on the right of + or -, a timestamp under *, a list under text) with <op> does not accept a <Shape> operand, giving + and - the shape of their left operand.",
      "rationale": "WG-5064 (Strata D-6540 to D-6543, CMS-1540 to CMS-1546): Strata's tenant rule language needs arithmetic, time windows and keys built from several values, and its two evaluators (Exegra's and the Rust specc generates) must agree bit for bit, which the range of 2^53-1 makes possible without a big-integer type. Evidence is the core tests: the shared case table holds every new operator with ordinary values, both ends of the range, overflow in each direction, nulls and type errors, for eval and for check. Supported, not verified: the Rust side reads the same table in Strata.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
        "validator"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "language-grapheme-emoji",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The primitive catalogue holds the grapheme and emoji functions (graphemes, is-emoji), and the language evaluator gives for them Strata's results over its shared case table: graphemes of a string is its count of extended grapheme clusters (UAX #29), so that a combining sequence, a ZWJ emoji sequence, a flag or a keycap is one; is-emoji of a string is true exactly when the string is one grapheme that is a keycap, a flag, a subdivision tag sequence, or ZWJ-joined parts each an emoji presentation or a text-default emoji followed by VS16 or a skin tone; a VS15 makes it not an emoji, and a lone regional indicator, a lone digit, a bare copyright, trademark or heart, two emoji and the empty string are not; a null operand gives null and an operand of another kind is an error.",
      "rationale": "WG-5603 (Strata WG-5597): Strata's tenant rule language gained graphemes and is-emoji, and Exegra's validation of Strata's specification refused them with language.primitive-unknown. Both are pinned to the Strata kernel's functions (crates/kernel/src/expr.rs, crates/model-core/src/tenant_rule_language.rs), so Exegra's evaluator must agree with Strata's on every case of Strata's table, which the core test mirrors. Supported, not verified: the emoji properties come from the JavaScript and Rust Unicode data of their own versions, so a character added by a later Unicode release could classify differently on the two sides.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
        "validator"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
        "method": "external-evidence"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "language-ancestor-of",
      "kind": "invariant",
      "statement": "The primitive catalogue holds ancestor-of, and the language evaluator gives for it Strata's results (WG-5510): ancestor_of(x, y, object, field) with x and y references and object and field string literals is true when x is y or one of its ancestors, the walk from y following the single reference field of the object type; it is false when the walk ends at a null field or an absent record; and it is an error, never a silent false, when the walk takes more than 32 hops, returns to a record it visited, or meets a record the lookup does not cover. Null on a null reference, an error on any other kind.",
      "rationale": "WG-5510 (Strata hierarchies and recursion): Strata's tenant rule language gains a bounded ancestor test over a self-reference, so that a policy can grant a manager read over a subtree and a comment can be tested against its thread. Exegra's validation of Strata's specification refuses an operator whose primitive is not in its catalogue, so the primitive enters the catalogue before Strata's specification uses it. Its semantics are the kernel's (crates/kernel/src/expr.rs, Expr::AncestorOf, ANCESTOR_HOPS = 32); Exegra's evaluator must agree with the kernel's on every case of the shared table in tests/core/language.test.js.",
      "subjects": [
        "generation-readiness",
        "validator"
      ],
      "assumptions": [
        "tests-exercise-shipped-modules",
        "recorded-results-reflect-current-code"
      ],
      "evaluate": {
